{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"alias":"unamicable-pygmyish-needletail","creation_channel":"cli","description":"Plan ACs in this repo keep being written against a misread of the very command they cite, so\nthey are unmeetable by any correct implementation and only surface at close, after the change\nhas already landed. Two distinct misreads are now confirmed; both belong in the plan-authoring\nguide (`rebar explain plan`, source `src/rebar/_guides/writing-a-passing-plan.md`) next to the\nexisting blocking checklist, not in a new document.\n\n## Misread 1 — `check_mechanism_delta.py --check` counts ADMITTED entries in `new=`\n\nThe summary line reads `active=648 new=28 increased=0 stale=0`. `new=` counts every mechanism\nabsent from the baseline, **including** those admitted by an in-tree `# mechanism-ok:` marker.\nOnly an *unadmitted* new mechanism fails the gate, and the gate reports that through its **exit\ncode**, not through the counter.\n\nMeasured 2026-09 on `origin/main` at `e21f3240cd0`: `--check` prints `new=27` and exits **0**.\nSo an AC reading \"`python scripts/check_mechanism_delta.py --check` reports `new=0`\" is false of\nthe repository's own default state and cannot be made true by implementing anything.\n\nThree tickets have now shipped that exact clause: `f5f5-51cd-...` AC5, `b939-bc68-1cb2-4ee9`\nAC2, and the adjacent generated-artifact count defects in `db2c-3a4b-...` / `25a4-1ab3-...`.\nEach cost a close-time investigation to establish that the clause, not the implementation, was\nwrong.\n\n## Misread 2 — `make verify` does not run `integration`-marked tests\n\n`make test` selects `not integration and not external`. A module whose `pytestmark` is\n`pytest.mark.integration` — `tests/integration/test_concurrency_regression.py`, 33 tests — is\ntherefore **not** executed by `make verify`. An AC whose only coverage claim is \"`make verify`\ngreen\" does not cover a change to those files.\n\n`b939-bc68-1cb2-4ee9` rewired 10 subprocess call sites in that module. `make verify` would have\npassed whether or not that rewiring worked; it was proven only by running the module explicitly\nwith `-m \"\"`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] AC1 `src/rebar/_guides/writing-a-passing-plan.md` tells plan authors to assert the\n      mechanism gate's **exit code** (or \"exits 0\" / \"no unadmitted new mechanism\"), names\n      `new=N` as counting admitted entries, and states the measured fact that `origin/main`\n      itself reports a nonzero `new`. `rebar explain plan` renders the new text.\n- [ ] AC2 The same guide tells plan authors that `make verify` excludes `integration`- and\n      `external`-marked tests, and that an AC touching such a module must name the explicit\n      command that runs it (`pytest <path> -m \"\"`) in addition to `make verify`.\n- [ ] AC3 `grep -n 'mechanism-ok\\|new=' src/rebar/_guides/writing-a-passing-plan.md` and\n      `grep -n 'integration' src/rebar/_guides/writing-a-passing-plan.md` each return at least\n      one hit, and `make verify` is green.","id":"6f24-3d47-b251-4905","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Plan-authoring guide lets ACs cite make verify and the mechanism gate in forms no implementation can satisfy"},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1793441363834739001,"uuid":"0934d454-7094-4e30-93c5-a8acee97ab62"}